<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Using an LLM to generate training data for another is among the most general ways to train one, and it
remains common practice. Some frontier teams have closed that route for their own models: what comes
back is an answer, or a short summary, without the detailed reasoning trace behind it. That is a little ironic: they were themselves built on distilling human knowledge, and distilling an open-source model is
still entirely routine; the restriction applies to their own outputs alone. Recent work recovers the
hidden reasoning by replaying encrypted blocks across models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panfilov et al. [2026]</a></cite>. It somehow
works, but it breaks the providers’ terms of service, so it is not something a product can be built on
long-term.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We built the alternative. We take the context and the teacher’s answer as input, and generate the
reasoning trace ourselves, in the style of the student’s own model family. We call the result a “family-native” trace. Training is two-stage,
supervised and then adversarial: a generator learns to regenerate the family’s own reasoning traces from their context and answer, and a critic learns how real a reasoning trace looks. Applied to a teacher’s answer, the generator writes one in the family’s style. A closed judge scores the family-native traces at 68% against 66% for the real ones, and at 82% once
the critic picks the best of four.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">We test the idea on five benchmarks with everything else held fixed. On general tasks the five training corpora are about the same; on coding, the generated reasoning beats Fable 5’s own thinking
content. With the same <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{,}500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3,500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{,}500</annotation></semantics></math> questions and the same teacher answers, HumanEval+ improves by <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.7</annotation></semantics></math>
points and MBPP+ by <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.1</annotation></semantics></math> at the final checkpoint, and the MBPP+ lead holds at every checkpoint.
Intuitively, we attribute this to the student and the family-native traces coming from the same model
family: the student can absorb them without unlearning its own habits, so adaptation costs less and
catastrophic forgetting is smaller.</p>
  
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.29680v1/fig_teaser.svg" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/206;" width="452" height="206"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Same answers, better traces.<span id="S0.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> HumanEval+ (left) and MBPP+ (right) across training. Against the model trained on Fable 5’s own traces, ours with Fable 5’s answers wins at nearly every checkpoint. Both of our models, with Fable 5 and with GPT-6.1-SOL as the teacher, are far better than the one trained with Qwen3.8-Max as teacher.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using an LLM to generate training data for another is among the most general ways to train one. A strong
model answers a set of questions, a weaker one is trained on those answers and, better still, on the
reasoning that led to them, and the gap closes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI [2025]</a></cite>. This is not a loophole. Training a model on another model’s output is how this field has advanced from the start.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Some labs have decided to stop this. The strongest models now return an answer, or a short summary of
their reasoning, and nothing more. The stated reasons are safety and intellectual property; the effect
is a moat, and the moat is the point. Others have tried to go around it by hacking the reasoning traces directly:
recent work shows that the encrypted reasoning blocks returned by providers are interchangeable across
models and can be replayed to recover a hidden reasoning trace verbatim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panfilov et al. [2026]</a></cite>. It works, but
it breaks the terms under which those APIs are sold, so it is not something to build on long term.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a legal alternative: do not recover the teacher’s reasoning: generate the missing reasoning
ourselves. We train a generator to regenerate the reasoning traces of the student’s own model family from their context and answer, and a critic to keep those reasoning traces indistinguishable from the family’s real ones. Applied to a teacher’s answer, the generator writes the reasoning that arrives at it, in the family’s style. The two are trained against each other, and at inference
the critic picks the best of several candidates, with no labels and nothing that violates a provider’s
terms. We call the result a family-native trace.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We train Qwen3.5-9B-Base on reasoning traces produced in different ways. On the two coding benchmarks,
replacing Fable 5’s reasoning trace with a family-native one raises pass@1 by <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.1</annotation></semantics></math> points at the final
checkpoint, and the MBPP+ lead holds at every checkpoint we measured; on the three general-knowledge
tasks, which do not test reasoning, the result is about the same. Reasoning traces synthesized from GPT-6.1-SOL’s
answers reach a similar level. Family-native traces also beat training on the family’s strongest model directly: with Qwen3.8-Max’s own reasoning traces and answers, the same student trails our corpus by <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>35</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35</annotation></semantics></math> points on the same two benchmarks. The teacher’s capability sets the ceiling for the student’s.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">One might be surprised that the generated reasoning traces beat Fable 5’s own. We attribute it to the source of the
form: a reasoning trace written by the student’s own family is data the student can absorb without unlearning its
own habits, so adaptation costs less and catastrophic forgetting is smaller than when the same capability
arrives in a foreign hand.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contribution is easy to state. Training on another model’s output is an old and open technique; the only line that matters is whether the data was obtained legally. We use the word
distillation without apology, and we stay on the right side of that line. The community can now learn
from the best models without asking permission and, on the evidence here, end up with a better
student than those models’ own reasoning traces would have produced.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.29680v1/Figures/fig_pipeline.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/199;" width="476" height="199" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">The pipeline.<span id="S2.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> A conversation is a system prompt, user turns, tool calls and
responses, an answer, and the reasoning trace that sits before the answer. Training the generator
(blue) takes the conversation with the reasoning trace removed and restores it as the target (green). The critic
(purple) is shown a real reasoning trace and a generated one and must say which is which. The generator is then
trained against the critic’s verdict (arc).</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Problem</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Method ‣ TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the object we work with, a conversation written as a message list. Let
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be the reasoning trace and let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be everything else: the system prompt, the user
turns, the answer, and any tool call or response. The generator models</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(R\mid S,U,A,T)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(R\mid S,U,A,T),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">the reasoning trace a model would write given the rest of the exchange.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training uses conversations from the student’s own family only. For the roughly ten thousand reasoning traces we
collected from Qwen3.8-Max, all five elements are present, so <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is supervised directly and no
teacher is involved. At inference we take <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S,U,A,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S,U,A,T</annotation></semantics></math> from a closed-source model, which supplies
everything except the reasoning trace, and sample the reasoning trace the family would have written. The teacher therefore
enters only at inference, and only as the source of those four inputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Supervised training of the generator</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The generator is a model from the Qwen family. It is fine-tuned on those ten thousand conversations to
maximize <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log P(R\mid S,U,A,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log P(R\mid S,U,A,T)</annotation></semantics></math>: the reasoning trace is removed from the input, everything else is kept, and
the loss falls on the reasoning trace alone. This is the blue path in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Method ‣ TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Two details carry
the design. The target is always a reasoning trace the family itself wrote, so the form is learned from data
rather than requested in a prompt. And the answer is part of the input and never part of the output, so
the model learns a derivation that <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ends</em> at <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> rather than a restatement of it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One consequence is worth stating, because it is easy to misread the result. The reasoning traces we train on were
written for the family’s own answers. When we later ask the generator for a reasoning trace to accompany a
teacher’s answer, it cannot be reproducing a reasoning trace it has seen. It is doing what it was trained to do:
writing a derivation that ends at that answer, in the family’s voice. We call the result a <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">family-native</em> trace. No provider gives us the real
reasoning trace anyway, and none is needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After this stage we ask an outside judge, GPT-6.1-SOL, for the probability that a reasoning trace is real. Real
family reasoning traces average <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.66</annotation></semantics></math>; the generator’s average <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.54" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.54</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.54</annotation></semantics></math>. That gap is what the rest of the section
addresses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Supervised training of the critic</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The critic is trained to tell real reasoning traces from generated ones, the purple path of
Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Method ‣ TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. For each of the ten thousand samples the generator produced one matched
negative, and training stops once the critic assigns the positive a probability at least <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> higher
than that negative, rather than driving the margin as far as it will go.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We made one mistake here that is worth recording. We assumed that a smaller model could judge a larger
one. It cannot: within a few hundred steps the critic fitted shortcuts, scored well on held-out pairs,
and gave the generator nothing to learn from. The critic is therefore at least as large as the
generator.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Adversarial co-training</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We then run five rounds of online co-training, the arc at the bottom of Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Method ‣ TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In
each round the generator produces <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> reasoning traces, which are shown to the critic alongside the
corresponding real reasoning traces. A reasoning trace that fools the critic becomes critic training data; a reasoning trace that
does not becomes a preference pair for the generator. At the end of each round we re-score a held-out set with GPT-6.1-SOL.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The score stops improving after five rounds, and we stop there. At that point the generator alone scores
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.68" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.68</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.68</annotation></semantics></math>, above the <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.66</annotation></semantics></math> of real reasoning traces, and selecting the best of four candidates with the critic
scores <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.82" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.82</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.82</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The point of this work is to break the moat of the closed-source model providers, so the test that
settles it is a simple one: is a student trained on family-native traces as good as a student trained on
the real ones? Everything in this section follows from that question. We hold the base model, the
training recipe and the budget fixed, vary only where the reasoning trace and the answer come from, and we simply want to see how the two students compare.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every student is fine-tuned from
<a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B-Base" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B-Base</a>, the pretrained
checkpoint, so that the only difference between two students is their training data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We distil from 5,000 Claude Code trajectories that carry Fable 5’s real reasoning traces and answers, at a 256K context window. The source is confidential, but the pool is fixed: every corpus below uses the same prompts and the same Fable 5 answers. From this single pool we build five training corpora that differ only in where the reasoning trace
and the answer come from.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S1, Fable 5 reasoning <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> answer.</span> The original data, used as is: for each of the 5,000 prompts, Fable 5’s real reasoning trace together with its own answer.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S2, family-native <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> Fable 5 answer.</span> As S1, with the reasoning trace replaced by a
family-native trace from the generator and critic of Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Method ‣ TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>; the answers are
unchanged. S1 and S2 therefore differ only in who wrote the reasoning trace.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S3, Qwen-Max reasoning <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> answer.</span> The prompts of S1 (all context) are
given to Qwen3.8-Max, the strongest legal model in the student’s own
family, and its reasoning traces and answers are used for training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S4, TraceInversion <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> Sol answer.</span> We have no GPT-6.1-SOL data carrying real reasoning traces, so
we mimic S3: we collect GPT-6.1-SOL’s answers on the same prompts and
synthesize a reasoning trace for each with <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TraceInversion</span> (<span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sol</span> below), the inversion method of
Zhang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [2026]</a></cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S5, family-native <math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> Sol answer.</span> As S2, applied to S4: our reasoning traces over GPT-6.1-SOL’s answers.
S4 and S5 share the answers and differ only in the synthesizer.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Teacher-forced SFT with the prompt masked from the loss. No truncation: every row
is under 262,144 tokens, and packed sequences may reach that length. Full parameters, BF16, AdamW with
weight decay <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and gradient clipping <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, learning rate <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> under a cosine schedule
with 5% warmup down to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, micro-batch <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, eight H20 GPUs with TP4/PP2 and sequence
parallelism, seed <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42</annotation></semantics></math>, no shuffling. One step is one packed-batch update. The adversarial stage adds
five rounds of online preference data, used with the standard DPO objective.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use three multiple-choice tasks, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge and BoolQ, to test
general ability in a non-reasoning mode, and two code benchmarks, HumanEval+ and MBPP+, to test coding
ability in a reasoning mode.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Main comparison</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Main comparison ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives the final checkpoint. The three knowledge benchmarks do not separate the five
corpora: every value sits within <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.6</annotation></semantics></math> points of every other, and no setting leads on all three. That is
what we expect of tasks that never ask for a reasoning trace, and we set them aside here. The code benchmarks are a different picture,
with a spread of more than sixty points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first surprise is that S2 beats S1. Replacing the teacher’s own reasoning trace with a family-native one adds
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.7</annotation></semantics></math> points on HumanEval+ and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.1</annotation></semantics></math> on MBPP+, and Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Main comparison ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the MBPP+ lead
holding at every checkpoint. S1 is the textbook recipe, built on the assumption that the strongest
available reasoning trace is the best training signal. On these two benchmarks it is not.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second surprise is S3. Qwen-Max is a far stronger model than the student, so we expected it to be
almost as good a teacher as Fable 5, perhaps a little behind. It is nowhere near: <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> points behind on
HumanEval+ and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>28</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28</annotation></semantics></math> on MBPP+. A capable teacher writing in its own hand transfers much less than a
stronger teacher whose answers are re-expressed in the student’s hand. Teacher capability turns out to
be necessary but not sufficient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">TraceInversion is the weakest of the five, at <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.66</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.61</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.61</annotation></semantics></math>, and this one did not surprise us once
we read its output. Its reasoning traces are recognisably synthetic: they narrate the solution in a generic voice,
use numbered steps the family never writes, and often restate the answer rather than derive it. The
score matches what a glance at the data already suggests.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">S5 closes the argument with a second teacher. Given exactly the same GPT-6.1-SOL answers as S4, our
reasoning traces reach <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>59.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.15</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60.05</annotation></semantics></math> against <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.66</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.61</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.61</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>55</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="53" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>53</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">53</annotation></semantics></math> points with nothing varying but
the synthesizer. Set beside S2, which shares the synthesizer and differs only in whose answers it writes,
S5 is <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> points behind on HumanEval+ and level on MBPP+. That is about the gap one would expect between
the two teachers themselves, and it matches the common view of how the two model families compare.</p>
</div>
