<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Further analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We also want to know how each corpus behaves over training, not only where it ends.
Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Main comparison ‣ 3 Evaluation ‣ TraceForge: Distilling with Your Own Thinking" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports every intermediate checkpoint on the two code benchmarks, and the
trajectories say more than the endpoints do.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">S1 is not stable. It reaches <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>66.46</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66.46</annotation></semantics></math> on HumanEval+ at step 600, falls to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33.54" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>33.54</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33.54</annotation></semantics></math> at step 1,200, and
recovers to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64.63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64.63</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64.63</annotation></semantics></math> by the end; we re-ran the 1,200-step evaluation twice to rule out a scoring error,
and the number held. A 33-point drop that repairs itself is not noise. Our reading is that the teacher’s
reasoning trace style sits far enough from the family’s own that fitting it moves the parameters a long way, and a
long move is a move that can overshoot.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">S2 and S5 go the other way. S2 rises steadily to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="68.29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>68.29</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">68.29</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>59.52</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.52</annotation></semantics></math>, and S5 rises at every single
checkpoint on both benchmarks, from <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.61</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.61</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10.32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.32</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="59.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>59.15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">59.15</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60.05</annotation></semantics></math>; on the average of the two,
S5 edges past S1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">S5 starts last and finishes near the front. It has the same reasoning traces as S2 and different answers, so the
answers are where the difference must come from. Qwen3.5’s mid-training is widely said to have used a lot
of Claude-family data and very little GPT-family data, which would make GPT-6.1-SOL’s answer style new to
the base model: it needs steps to adapt to it before the reasoning trace can help. The reasoning trace style is not new, which
is why the climb, once it starts, never reverses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We also have some interesting findings on S3 and S4. Both had their moment and both ended badly: S3
reaches <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48.78" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>48.78</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48.78</annotation></semantics></math> on HumanEval+ inside the first 300 steps and never gets back, and S4 peaks at <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>21.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21.95</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>42.59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42.59</annotation></semantics></math> at step 900 before falling to <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.66</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.66</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.61" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6.61</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.61</annotation></semantics></math>. Reading their outputs, the cause is not subtle.
A growing share of the reasoning traces are dead loops or meaningless repetition: a sentence comes back with small
variations, or the model keeps re-deriving a result it already has, and the longer the run the more of it
there is.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This matches a piece of commonsense among foundation-model teams. Distilling from a distilled model, which people call
“breathing second-hand smoke”, may result in a student that tends to fall into dead loops while it thinks;
in contrast, train on Claude or GPT directly and the problem rarely appears. Intuitively, we guess what happened to S3 and S4 is, in some measure,
explained by that one habit. S1, which takes its reasoning traces and answers from Fable 5 directly, never shows the pattern. Finally, we were pleased to find that our family-native traces do not produce it at all.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The two tables together settle the comparison. A family-native trace is not merely a legal
substitute for a real one. On the comparison that isolates the reasoning trace, with questions and answers held
fixed, <span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">it is even the better teacher</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discussion and Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The result is easy to state. Our reasoning traces beat the strongest model we are allowed to distil from,
and they beat it by a wide margin: against Qwen-Max’s own reasoning traces and answers we are <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>35</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35</annotation></semantics></math>
points ahead. They also edge past the teacher’s own reasoning traces, by <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.1</annotation></semantics></math> points with the
questions and answers held fixed. A comparison from schooling fits. The best student is not produced by the best teacher alone, but by that teacher teaching in the way the student learns best. Our traces are
that lesson plan: the strongest teacher’s content, delivered in a way that students are familiar with.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Neither the generator nor the critic has ever seen a Claude or an OpenAI token. Every training example comes from Qwen3.8-Max, a model whose terms permit this
use, so the pipeline violates no provider’s agreement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One more option, for teams that still see risk in handling Claude or OpenAI answers at all. Generate the
family-native trace, hand it to the strongest legally-distillable model in the same family such as Qwen3.8-Max, and let that
model write the answer. Then train on the family-native trace and that answer together. No Claude or
OpenAI token is backpropagated at any point, which removes both the legal exposure and the provenance
question: <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the pipeline yields data that is, on every count, the family’s own</span>. We have run this variant, and it performs essentially the same as S2.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Learning from reasoning traces.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reasoning traces provide supervision beyond final answers.
Distilling Step-by-Step uses teacher-generated rationales as an additional training target for smaller
task-specific models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsieh et al. [2023]</a></cite>; Orca learns from explanation traces generated by larger
models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mukherjee et al. [2023]</a></cite>; and DeepSeek-R1 transfers reasoning capabilities to smaller models through
curated reasoning data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI [2025]</a></cite>. These works motivate training on intermediate reasoning. Our
question concerns its source and form: when a teacher’s answer is fixed, can a trace synthesized in the
student’s model-family style teach more effectively than the teacher’s original trace?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Recovering and synthesizing hidden reasoning.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Panfilov et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panfilov et al. [2026]</a></cite>
recover protected reasoning by transferring encrypted blocks across models. Synthesis takes a different
route: Trace Inversion learns to generate reasoning from inputs, answers and optional
summaries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. [2026]</a></cite>, while REER works backwards from known-good solutions for
open-ended generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. [2026]</a></cite>. Trace Inversion already shows that synthesized traces can
outperform answer-only training and, in some settings, original traces; it also reports weaker coding
results associated with style and format mismatch. TraceForge instead trains its generator on the
student family’s own traces and uses adversarial feedback from a family-trace critic. Our S1/S2
comparison holds Fable 5’s answers fixed and replaces only the trace source; S4/S5 holds GPT-6.1-SOL’s
answers fixed and compares the two synthesis pipelines. These comparisons establish downstream
performance differences, but do not isolate family style from every other difference between the pipelines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Teacher–student distribution differences.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MiniLLM changes the distillation objective to
reverse KL to better account for the student’s approximation of a generative teacher
distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. [2023]</a></cite>. Our intervention is in the training text: we retain an answer from a
strong teacher while synthesizing its accompanying reasoning in a family-native form. This separates
the answer source from the trace source. S3, which uses Qwen3.8-Max’s own answers and traces, is a
distinct baseline; its performance should not be read as an ablation of trace style with answers held fixed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Selection, verification and rewriting.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">STaR bootstraps reasoning by retaining successful
solutions and using correct answers to guide further rationale generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zelikman et al. [2022]</a></cite>.
Process supervision trains verifiers with step-level correctness labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al. [2023]</a></cite>, and
self-consistency aggregates sampled reasoning paths by answer agreement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. [2022]</a></cite>.
Our critic instead learns to distinguish genuine family traces from generated ones. It supports both
generator training and best-of-four selection without step-level correctness labels. Its authenticity
score and the independent judge’s score measure resemblance to family traces; downstream benchmark
performance is evaluated separately.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Adversarial training and the reconstruction target.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Self-play fine-tuning contrasts model
outputs with human demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. [2024]</a></cite>, and DPO learns from preference
pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. [2023]</a></cite>. We use a family-trace critic to construct online preference data for the
generator. Instruction backtranslation reconstructs an instruction from a response <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. [2023]</a></cite>;
our reconstruction target is the reasoning trace conditioned on the conversation and answer. Finally,
written reasoning need not faithfully reveal the computation that produced an
answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al. [2023]</a></cite>. Our goal is a useful family-native training trace, rather than a
verbatim reconstruction of the teacher’s hidden reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
